magic: the weekly gathering where the old lays of the valley are sung, and apprentices want to set them anew
scene: Every Thursday a dozen people meet in the back room of the community hall to sing the town's old songs, and {N} has become the one who keeps them going. Three newcomers want to set some of them to new tunes, with guitars. The oldest singer, {elder}, says they would not be the songs any more. The harvest night, where they sing for the town, is next month.
scene tribal: On the nights of the old songs the clan gathers at the fire, and {N} has become the one who keeps them going. Some of the young want to sing them faster, to new drums. {elder}, the oldest singer, says they would not be the songs any more. The harvest feast is a moon away.
scene magic: Every week a dozen folk meet in the inn's back room to sing the valley's old lays, and {N} has become the one who keeps them going. Three apprentices of the music guild want to set some anew, in the court's manner. {elder} says they would not be the lays any more. The harvest feast is next month.
